1
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:29,980
community community

2
00:00:29,981 --> 00:00:32,080
to traffic all day.

3
00:00:32,180 --> 00:00:35,520
New York City was holding a $3 million birthday party

4
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:41,620
to celebrate the centennial of its oldest, most famous bridge.

5
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:45,220
Yet even that display couldn't match the triumph of May 24,

6
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:48,440
1883, when the bridge first opened,

7
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:51,940
linking the then independent cities of Brooklyn and New

8
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,640
York.

9
00:00:53,740 --> 00:00:55,580
The schools and the shops were closed

10
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,580
because Mayor Seth Lowe, who later

11
00:00:57,581 --> 00:01:01,020
who later became the first mayor of New York City combined,

12
00:01:01,021 --> 00:01:03,280
declared it a holiday.

13
00:01:03,281 --> 00:01:06,180
It was a city holiday, and Brooklyn was very, very proud

14
00:01:06,181 --> 00:01:07,820
of this bridge.

15
00:01:07,821 --> 00:01:10,520
By overcoming the geographical separation

16
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:13,920
between Brooklyn and Manhattan, the Brooklyn Bridge

17
00:01:13,921 --> 00:01:19,800
led to the 1898 merger that created greater New York.

18
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:22,840
Before the bridge, Brooklyn had been a rural community,

19
00:01:22,841 --> 00:01:25,180
even though it had the third largest population

20
00:01:25,181 --> 00:01:26,500
in the country.

21
00:01:26,501 --> 00:01:28,160
led people to settle there.

22
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,740
But all the jobs were across the East River

23
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:34,100
in the thriving business center of New York.

24
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,440
By 1868, there were over 50 million commuters a year.

25
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:46,160
There has to be a demand for the service of a bridge.

26
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:52,300
At that time, New York and Brooklyn were growing very

27
00:01:52,301 --> 00:01:57,040
very rapidly, but the only way you could get across was by

28
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,040
ferry, which was not a very reliable system

29
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:05,040
with the river froze over or storms and things.

30
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:07,940
Since the early 1800s, Brooklyn had

31
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:12,260
wanted safer, more reliable transportation to New York.

32
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,360
But the physical obstacles seemed insurmountable.

33
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:19,060
A bridge across the East River would be over a mile long,

34
00:02:19,061 --> 00:02:22,260
more than twice as long as any existing bridge.

35
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,700
It would have to be exceptionally strong,

36
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:26,760
because the East River is actually

37
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:30,500
a turbulent saltwater channel rising and falling

38
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,500
with the tides of the Atlantic Ocean.

39
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,820
Finally, the bridge would have to be high enough

40
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:39,120
to permit ocean-going vessels to pass underneath.

41
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:44,120
Such a bridge seemed unbuildable, but not to John Roebling.

42
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:48,520
During the winter of 1852, Roebling, a civil engineer,

43
00:02:48,521 --> 00:02:52,520
was stranded aboard an ice-clogged ferry on the East River.

44
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:55,320
That experience ignited his determination

45
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:58,260
to build a bridge across the river.

46
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:01,760
45 years earlier, John Roebling had been born

47
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:03,760
in Mülhausen, Germany.

48
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:06,680
Possessing a brilliant analytical mind,

49
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:09,680
he went to the Polytechnic Institute in Berlin,

50
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:15,080
where he studied mathematics, architecture, and hydraulics.

51
00:03:15,081 --> 00:03:18,380
At 25, John Roebling sailed to Pennsylvania

52
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:21,820
to found a community of German immigrants.

53
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:25,320
He married Johanna Herting, another colonist.

54
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:27,820
Roebling called his town Saxenburg

55
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:30,120
and saw it as a kind of utopia.

56
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:33,540
He also saw himself as a kind of farmer.

57
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,900
But his skills as a scholar and scientist

58
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,040
were unsuited to agriculture, and the farming went poorly.

59
00:03:41,041 --> 00:03:45,140
In 1837, Roebling became an American citizen,

60
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:49,740
but remained personally and professionally unfulfilled,

61
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,680
a visionary who had not yet found his one true calling.

62
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,800
He returned to engineering and became a builder of river

63
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:01,760
locks and a student of metals in Western Pennsylvania,

64
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:04,160
where the nation's iron and steel industries

65
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:07,200
were beginning to exist.

66
00:04:07,201 --> 00:04:11,240
There he studied the Portage Railroad, a system for hauling

67
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:15,340
canal boats over the Allegheny Mountains.

68
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:19,080
Roebling was dissatisfied with the hemp cables used to tow

69
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:21,320
boats on the Portage Railroad.

70
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:24,880
The ropes wore out rapidly or snapped, sometimes

71
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:26,780
with fatal results.

72
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:29,720
Roebling set to work on a solution.

73
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:33,960
His laboratory was the meadow behind the church in Saxenburg.

74
00:04:33,961 --> 00:04:42,960
There he spent the summer of 1841 splicing and winding iron wire into a 600-foot long cable.

75
00:04:42,961 --> 00:04:46,960
Roebling's iron cable became a gold mine.

76
00:04:46,961 --> 00:04:54,960
Orders flowed in from canal systems, makers of dredging equipment and pile drivers, coal mines,

77
00:04:54,961 --> 00:05:00,960
all the evolving technologies that would reshape and redefine America.

78
00:05:00,961 --> 00:05:06,960
In 1844, Roebling won his first real commission as an engineer.

79
00:05:06,961 --> 00:05:13,960
Nine months later, he delivered the world's first suspension aqueduct over Pittsburgh's Allegheny River.

80
00:05:13,961 --> 00:05:21,960
The aqueduct supported 2,000 tons of water on which floated a procession of canal barges.

81
00:05:21,961 --> 00:05:26,960
By 1850, Roebling had built six suspended structures.

82
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:33,660
Each depended on Roebling's wire rope, now produced by his own factory in Trenton, New Jersey.

83
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:44,420
From 1851 to 1855, Roebling was immersed in building a railway suspension bridge over the daunting Niagara Gorge.

84
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:50,980
This bridge proved that a light suspension structure could support the heavy weight of a railroad.

85
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:55,660
Roebling became the greatest bridge builder of his time.

86
00:05:55,661 --> 00:06:00,660
His passion for the suspension structure led to its preeminence in America.

87
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:04,660
Today, there are more suspension bridges in the United States

88
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,600
than in all the rest of the world.

89
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:13,220
The appeal of suspension bridges lies in their ability to span great distances

90
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:15,720
without intermediate piers.

91
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:19,720
They also require less material than other bridges.

92
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:23,660
Prior to Roebling, suspension bridges were out of favor

93
00:06:23,661 --> 00:06:29,780
of favor because so many had collapsed. But Roebling's bridges never fell due to

94
00:06:29,781 --> 00:06:33,100
his scrupulous attention to detail.

95
00:06:33,101 --> 00:06:42,140
He was successful at building the bridges because he was he was a great

96
00:06:42,141 --> 00:06:51,980
innovator but he did it very carefully. He did dramatic things but he did them

97
00:06:51,981 --> 00:06:53,780
meticulously.

98
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:56,680
He did not jump off onto something

99
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:01,420
that he did not think out thoroughly and carefully.

100
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:05,320
After Niagara, Roebling designed the Cincinnati Covington

101
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:08,200
Bridge, which became the world's longest bridge

102
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,960
when completed under the supervision of John Roebling's

103
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:14,640
son, Washington, in 1867.

104
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:16,840
By then, John Roebling was struggling

105
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:19,380
with severe depression.

106
00:07:19,381 --> 00:07:22,480
A reaction to Abraham Lincoln's assassination

107
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,980
and the death of his own wife, Johanna.

108
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,420
His chief comforts were the seances

109
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,920
at which he summoned Johanna's spirit,

110
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:34,620
along with those of other departed family members.

111
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,440
The one bright ray to penetrate Roebling's gloom

112
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:41,640
was his plan for an East River Bridge.

113
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:44,540
He claimed it would be the greatest engineering work

114
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:48,080
of the continent and the age.

115
00:07:48,081 --> 00:07:51,720
Roebling envisioned a bridge with one central span,

116
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:56,080
held aloft by four cables strung from two towers.

117
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:59,260
The cables were secured to masonry anchorages,

118
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:03,600
seven stories high and as large as a city block,

119
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:06,600
one anchorage on each shore.

120
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:12,340
The bridge would be dominated by 268-foot high twin towers.

121
00:08:12,440 --> 00:08:16,480
Each would contain two glorious Gothic arches,

122
00:08:16,481 --> 00:08:23,720
over 100 feet high, shaped like elongated cathedral windows.

123
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:29,620
The structure has a very sacred feeling to it,

124
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:31,820
a sacred dimension to it, essentially because it was

125
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:35,860
built in a very Gothic style that was influenced by, I

126
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:39,200
believe, the structure of churches.

127
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:42,200
Roebling designed a two-level bridge.

128
00:08:42,300 --> 00:08:44,840
The lower level was for vehicles.

129
00:08:44,841 --> 00:08:47,940
The top level was for pedestrians.

130
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:54,380
Even today, no other bridge in the world has anything like it.

131
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:58,260
In this case, no one asked Roebling to build a bridge.

132
00:08:58,360 --> 00:08:59,880
He wanted to build that bridge.

133
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,960
He felt it was needed there.

134
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,120
And his type of bridge would be the most practical, maybe

135
00:09:05,220 --> 00:09:08,000
the only practical way to do it.

136
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:14,040
And so he actually came to New York and promoted the bridge.

137
00:09:14,041 --> 00:09:16,740
Responding to Roebling's passion,

138
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:18,540
the state government in Albany

139
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:23,780
chartered the New York Bridge Company in December 1867.

140
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:26,680
Appointed chief engineer, John Roebling,

141
00:09:26,780 --> 00:09:30,180
but his project was immediately stalled by objections.

142
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,700
Roebling's most challenged innovation

143
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:36,800
was his plan to use steel rather than iron cables.

144
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:40,540
Roebling felt steel would provide stronger support,

145
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:43,340
but no bridge had ever been made of steel,

146
00:09:43,341 --> 00:09:46,640
And Roebling, for all his fame and brilliance,

147
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:49,580
was bitterly derided.

148
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:54,720
At 63, John Roebling was fed up with opposition.

149
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:57,120
The Union Pacific was completing a railroad

150
00:09:57,220 --> 00:10:01,700
to connect towns and cities from coast to coast.

151
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:04,560
Roebling saw his new bridge as the final link

152
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:08,040
in the same transcontinental chain,

153
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:13,340
the eastern outpost of manifest destiny in granite and steel.

154
00:10:13,341 --> 00:10:16,940
It was galling that the biggest challenge to Roebling's vision

155
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:22,080
of America came from its own Congress.

156
00:10:22,180 --> 00:10:24,480
Roebling had to meet federal requirements,

157
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:26,980
or the work that should rightfully be his noblest

158
00:10:27,080 --> 00:10:31,000
or most exalted contribution to the thriving industrial age

159
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:32,400
would be doomed.

160
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,140
The most strident critics of the Brooklyn Bridge

161
00:10:42,141 --> 00:10:47,103
Brooklyn Bridge were leveled by John Roebling with one brilliant stroke.

162
00:10:47,104 --> 00:10:48,620
Roebling, the bridge's

163
00:10:48,621 --> 00:10:53,572
designer, led his adversaries on a triumphant tour of his previous triumphs.

164
00:10:53,573 --> 00:10:54,940
The critics caved in.

165
00:10:55,820 --> 00:11:02,940
On June 21, 1869, Congress granted Roebling the right to build the Brooklyn Bridge.

166
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:08,747
Roebling's plan called for five phases of construction.

167
00:11:08,748 --> 00:11:11,260
First, two massive empty boxes

168
00:11:11,261 --> 00:11:14,520
known as caissons would be sunk to the bottom of the East River

169
00:11:14,521 --> 00:11:16,700
and filled with concrete.

170
00:11:16,701 --> 00:11:20,400
The caissons would become the foundations for the second stage,

171
00:11:20,401 --> 00:11:23,200
erecting the bridge's twin towers.

172
00:11:23,201 --> 00:11:26,700
In the third phase, two limestone and concrete anchorages

173
00:11:26,701 --> 00:11:31,860
would be built on shore, one in Brooklyn, the other in New York.

174
00:11:31,861 --> 00:11:34,700
The fourth stage of suspension bridge construction

175
00:11:34,701 --> 00:11:37,760
was the spinning of cables from one anchorage

176
00:11:37,761 --> 00:11:40,700
across the tops of the towers to the anchorage

177
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:42,660
on the other shore.

178
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:45,960
Finally, the cables would support a framework on which

179
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:49,500
would rest the bridge's roadway.

180
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:53,000
While he designed the bridge, Roebling's closest confidant

181
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:58,180
was his son Washington, his partner in the bridge project.

182
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:02,080
Washington's training as an engineer began at 17,

183
00:12:02,180 --> 00:12:04,960
when his domineering father packed him off to Rensselaer

184
00:12:04,961 --> 00:12:09,300
Polytechnic Institute in Troy, New York.

185
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:12,500
Washington was working at the family wire mill in Trenton

186
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:14,600
when the Civil War broke out.

187
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:17,840
Next morning, he enlisted.

188
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:20,800
That wartime experience gave him confidence

189
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:23,720
in his ability to lead.

190
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:28,020
He became a colonel on the staff of General G.K. Warren.

191
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:30,960
He fell in love with Warren's sister, Emily,

192
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:34,320
and grew a beard to please her.

193
00:12:34,321 --> 00:12:38,260
To Washington's surprise, his difficult father liked Emily

194
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:41,620
and appreciated her intelligence and devotion,

195
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:44,260
qualities that would prove essential in the battle

196
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:47,260
to complete the Brooklyn Bridge.

197
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:51,600
Washington and Emily were married in January 1865,

198
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:54,300
after Washington resigned from the army.

199
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:59,320
Their only child, John, was born in 1868.

200
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:01,920
If Washington lacked his father's genius,

201
00:13:01,921 --> 00:13:05,760
He was just as much a perfectionist as an engineer.

202
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:08,860
Both Roeblings began work on the bridge three days

203
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:12,160
after they received the go-ahead from Congress.

204
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:15,160
They went to the Fulton Ferry Slip to survey the site

205
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:17,360
of the Brooklyn Tower.

206
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:21,580
John Roebling marched about, bellowing calculations.

207
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:23,680
When he reached the edge of the ferry slip,

208
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:28,020
he stumbled into some debris and imprisoned his right foot.

209
00:13:28,120 --> 00:13:31,220
Just then, a ferry smashed against the slip,

210
00:13:31,221 --> 00:13:34,120
crushing Roebling's trapped toes.

211
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:37,760
He continued shouting orders until he passed out.

212
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:42,060
Ironically, Iron John Roebling became a victim of the fairies

213
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:45,260
he was trying to eliminate.

214
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:47,840
Washington rushed his father to a doctor,

215
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:50,800
who amputated the toes without anesthetic

216
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:52,740
by Roebling's request.

217
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:56,240
Roebling then fired the doctor and stubbornly treated his foot

218
00:13:56,340 --> 00:14:01,220
with water only, believing water was nature's own remedy.

219
00:14:01,221 --> 00:14:05,160
He had a very dangerous situation with his toes.

220
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:07,420
He had to have them amputated.

221
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:10,060
He was going to bathe them in hot water,

222
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:11,660
and that would cure the toes.

223
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:15,960
Gangrene set in, and tetanus eventually set in.

224
00:14:16,060 --> 00:14:19,480
Roebling had committed a typically headstrong act.

225
00:14:19,580 --> 00:14:22,540
This time, it was a hideous mistake.

226
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:26,140
Had Roebling obeyed the doctor's orders, he would have lived.

227
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:31,220
Instead, he died of tetanus three weeks after the accident.

228
00:14:31,221 --> 00:14:35,060
Following the biggest funeral in Trenton history,

229
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:38,760
John Roebling was buried near the Wire Empire he founded.

230
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:45,060
Flags flew at half-mast all over Brooklyn.

231
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:47,640
How would the bridge be built without John Roebling,

232
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:49,480
its guiding genius?

233
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:52,200
Only one man was equipped for the challenge,

234
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:55,180
but he would be taking on a gargantuan task that

235
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:57,840
would nearly kill him too.

236
00:14:57,940 --> 00:14:59,880
A month after John Roebling's death,

237
00:14:59,881 --> 00:15:05,380
his 32-year-old son Washington was made chief engineer.

238
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:08,860
Except for some bridges he built for the Union Army,

239
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:12,200
Washington had never been in charge alone.

240
00:15:12,300 --> 00:15:16,000
Now he would be trusted to implement every untested step

241
00:15:16,100 --> 00:15:18,140
of his father's vision.

242
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:21,560
He was bound to make mistakes because the Brooklyn Bridge

243
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:24,840
design was so revolutionary, there were no precedents

244
00:15:24,940 --> 00:15:26,440
to follow.

245
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:28,940
Without his perseverance, Washington

246
00:15:28,941 --> 00:15:31,180
Washington would have been lost.

247
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,680
But it was his willingness to work side by side with his men

248
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:36,680
that would prove essential in the first stage

249
00:15:36,780 --> 00:15:39,280
of the construction process, the sinking

250
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:41,960
of the pneumatic caissons.

251
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:45,460
The danger of this work could not be underestimated.

252
00:15:45,560 --> 00:15:48,700
Washington had studied caissons, but never used them.

253
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:52,100
It would take three years and as many as a dozen deaths

254
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:56,000
before the caissons were securely in place.

255
00:15:56,001 --> 00:16:01,500
On May 3, 1870, the Brooklyn caisson was towed to its site.

256
00:16:01,501 --> 00:16:09,640
It cost over $100,000 and weighed 3,000 tons, or 1,000 tons more than the biggest clipper

257
00:16:09,641 --> 00:16:12,180
ship of the era.

258
00:16:12,181 --> 00:16:16,760
Designed to serve as the foundation for the bridge's massive towers, the caissons were

259
00:16:16,761 --> 00:16:23,060
enormous iron-reinforced wooden boxes open at the bottom.

260
00:16:23,061 --> 00:16:26,500
the caissons would be sunk to the muddy riverbed.

261
00:16:26,501 --> 00:16:30,440
But in order to support the tremendous load of the towers,

262
00:16:30,441 --> 00:16:33,400
the caissons would need to descend further

263
00:16:33,401 --> 00:16:37,380
beneath the riverbed, all the way down to the bedrock

264
00:16:37,381 --> 00:16:39,520
of the river bottom.

265
00:16:39,521 --> 00:16:42,380
The backbreaking work of excavating the mud and rock

266
00:16:42,381 --> 00:16:46,480
was laid upon hundreds of low-paid laborers.

267
00:16:46,481 --> 00:16:48,960
Workers entered the caisson through an airlock

268
00:16:48,961 --> 00:16:51,160
built into its roof.

269
00:16:51,161 --> 00:16:54,760
From there, they descended by elevator and ladder

270
00:16:54,860 --> 00:16:56,960
into the pressurized working chamber

271
00:16:57,060 --> 00:16:59,760
at the bottom of the caisson.

272
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:02,700
Along the rim of the caisson's open base

273
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:04,740
was a cutting edge.

274
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:07,280
The cutting edge was designed to pierce the mud

275
00:17:07,380 --> 00:17:09,940
through which the caisson descended.

276
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:12,180
As mud seeped into the working chambers,

277
00:17:12,280 --> 00:17:14,580
the men shoveled it into wheelbarrows.

278
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:16,980
The more mud they excavated and shoveled,

279
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:20,120
the deeper the caisson would sink.

280
00:17:20,121 --> 00:17:23,820
The men placed the excavated material in pits at the base

281
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:27,620
of two water shafts.

282
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:31,640
The material was hauled up and out by dredge buckets dropped

283
00:17:31,740 --> 00:17:34,740
down from above.

284
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:37,340
This process of digging would continue

285
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:40,400
until the caisson reached the bedrock of the river bottom.

286
00:17:43,740 --> 00:17:45,820
Life in the caisson was brutal.

287
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:49,620
Men had to perform hard labor in unbearable heat.

288
00:17:49,621 --> 00:17:53,660
anywhere from 80 to 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

289
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:55,860
They could barely see what they were digging,

290
00:17:55,960 --> 00:17:59,120
because any light except the dim calcium lamps

291
00:17:59,220 --> 00:18:01,700
would have been too flammable.

292
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:04,060
To most laborers, the working chambers

293
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:07,200
with their deep shadows and confusing noises

294
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:08,840
resembled Dante's Inferno.

295
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:15,920
The job grew even harder when the caisson encountered boulders.

296
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:18,720
At 20 feet below sea level, the boulders

297
00:18:18,721 --> 00:18:23,120
were so thick the caisson seamed and trapped.

298
00:18:23,220 --> 00:18:26,860
Washington knew there was only one solution,

299
00:18:26,960 --> 00:18:30,600
blast the boulders, despite the risk to the caisson's

300
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:32,840
pressurized air.

301
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,400
After extensive tests, Washington

302
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:40,400
discovered blasting could be done safely with rifle powder.

303
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:45,220
Excavation speeded up, but the work grew more unnerving.

304
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:48,320
Worst of all were blowouts, loud,

305
00:18:48,321 --> 00:18:53,820
terrifying explosions caused by changes in air pressure.

306
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:58,260
The so-called Great Blowout occurred one Sunday

307
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:03,660
when an explosion shot mud and rocks 50 feet into the air.

308
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:06,280
Miraculously, there were no injuries,

309
00:19:06,380 --> 00:19:09,780
because Washington let his men take Sundays off.

310
00:19:09,880 --> 00:19:13,480
No one had been inside the caisson.

311
00:19:13,580 --> 00:19:16,320
The men escaped this disaster, but still

312
00:19:16,321 --> 00:19:20,260
being suffocated, crushed, and drowned.

313
00:19:20,360 --> 00:19:24,400
It took 264 workers to man the caisson shifts,

314
00:19:24,500 --> 00:19:29,860
yet the work was so dreaded that 100 men quit each week.

315
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:33,800
The workers, Irish, German, and Italian immigrants,

316
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:37,180
were quickly replaced by other immigrants more willing

317
00:19:37,280 --> 00:19:39,580
to endure the terrors of the caisson

318
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:43,820
for a steady salary of $2 a day.

319
00:19:43,821 --> 00:19:47,560
Yet the biggest threat to health was the mysterious malady

320
00:19:47,660 --> 00:19:51,300
known as caisson disease, or the bends.

321
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,440
Symptoms included vomiting, excruciating cramps,

322
00:19:55,540 --> 00:19:57,700
and paralysis.

323
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:01,180
As scuba divers and astronauts who breathe pressurized air

324
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:03,900
are taught today, the bends are caused

325
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,880
by excessively rapid decompression.

326
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:10,320
This releases nitrogen blockages into the bloodstream,

327
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:13,820
preventing oxygen from reaching body tissue.

328
00:20:13,821 --> 00:20:19,060
Tragically, no one in the 1870s knew what caused the bends.

329
00:20:19,160 --> 00:20:22,100
As many as 1 3rd of the caisson workers

330
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:25,500
were severely stricken by this affliction.

331
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:28,600
The more quickly a man emerged from prolonged stays

332
00:20:28,700 --> 00:20:32,300
at great depth, the greater the risk, which

333
00:20:32,400 --> 00:20:36,680
made Washington Roebling, who always worked alongside his men,

334
00:20:36,780 --> 00:20:40,620
a prime candidate for the disease.

335
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:43,580
Washington suffered his first attack of the men

336
00:20:43,581 --> 00:20:47,580
the night of September 28, 1869.

337
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:49,880
Fire erupted in the caisson.

338
00:20:49,980 --> 00:20:53,720
It was detected only after it burned through the roof.

339
00:20:53,820 --> 00:20:56,220
Washington arrived right away.

340
00:20:56,320 --> 00:21:00,160
But by 5 a.m. the next morning, the fire seemed over.

341
00:21:00,260 --> 00:21:03,940
Moments later, after 20 hours in the caisson,

342
00:21:04,040 --> 00:21:08,240
Washington collapsed and was rushed through the airlock.

343
00:21:08,340 --> 00:21:11,500
Reaching the surface, he doubled over in agony.

344
00:21:11,501 --> 00:21:15,600
He was rushed home and recovered for now.

345
00:21:15,700 --> 00:21:18,700
When fire was discovered again at 8 a.m.,

346
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:22,940
Washington took no chances and ordered the Brooklyn caisson

347
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:25,140
flooded for two days.

348
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:28,560
Repairs set the project back three months.

349
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:33,160
But by mid-March 1871, after a 10-month descent,

350
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:38,160
the Brooklyn caisson finally reached bedrock at 45 feet.

351
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:44,200
The New York caisson was launched on May 8, 1871.

352
00:21:44,300 --> 00:21:46,400
It encountered no boulders.

353
00:21:46,500 --> 00:21:51,400
Instead, Washington faced a new problem, endless sand.

354
00:21:51,500 --> 00:21:55,920
How long would it be before this caisson reached bedrock?

355
00:21:56,020 --> 00:21:59,340
By late April, almost a year after launching,

356
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:04,260
the caisson was 70 feet below sea level and still descending.

357
00:22:04,261 --> 00:22:07,960
At this great depth, most of the men suffered severely

358
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:09,960
from the bends.

359
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:13,600
Washington reduced the eight-hour work shift to five hours,

360
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:15,400
but it did no good.

361
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:18,700
Three men died of the disease the following week.

362
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:21,820
These were only the recorded deaths.

363
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:24,320
Kheisson workers believed there were more.

364
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:30,520
On May 8, 1872, the entire Kheisson force went on strike.

365
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:33,560
Yet three days later, most men returned

366
00:22:33,561 --> 00:22:41,060
already feeling the pinch of poverty and lured by a raise to $2.75 a day.

367
00:22:41,160 --> 00:22:44,140
Attacks of the Benz increased.

368
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:48,000
Washington Roebling now feared it would take another year,

369
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:51,940
another million dollars, and another hundred lives

370
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:55,680
before the New York caisson reached bedrock.

371
00:22:55,780 --> 00:23:00,180
On May 18, three more men died of the Benz.

372
00:23:00,181 --> 00:23:04,420
That same day, Washington ordered the digging to stop.

373
00:23:04,520 --> 00:23:08,920
The New York caisson would rest forever at a depth of 78

374
00:23:09,020 --> 00:23:12,920
and 1 1⁄2 feet on a bed of sand.

375
00:23:13,020 --> 00:23:16,000
The decision was less of a gamble than many believed.

376
00:23:16,100 --> 00:23:19,140
The sand here was crusted solid, hard enough

377
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:21,700
to batter iron tools.

378
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:25,380
A few nights later, after inspecting the concrete poured

379
00:23:25,480 --> 00:23:28,540
into the caisson, Washington collapsed again

380
00:23:28,541 --> 00:23:30,540
again from the bends.

381
00:23:30,640 --> 00:23:33,340
For several days, he lay near death.

382
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:37,240
But he survived by sheer will and went back to work,

383
00:23:37,340 --> 00:23:41,180
where he endured new attacks every week until the caisson

384
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:43,880
was finally finished.

385
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:46,600
Washington never regained his health.

386
00:23:46,700 --> 00:23:49,600
He withdrew to the Roebling home base in Trenton,

387
00:23:49,700 --> 00:23:51,400
a permanent invalid.

388
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:54,300
He suffered savage headaches and pains.

389
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:58,300
His eyesight was too dim for him to read or write.

390
00:23:58,301 --> 00:24:01,100
Hounded by visions of his own death,

391
00:24:01,200 --> 00:24:05,540
he would not return to the bridge during its construction.

392
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:09,040
Meanwhile, work on the bridge continued.

393
00:24:09,140 --> 00:24:12,380
A trusted guardian angel kept the project moving

394
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:17,520
according to Washington's plans, a guardian angel named

395
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:18,920
Emily Roebling.

396
00:24:18,921 --> 00:24:29,660
It took four years to build each of the strong, massive towers

397
00:24:29,760 --> 00:24:31,260
of the Brooklyn Bridge.

398
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:36,040
Beginning in 1871, granite blocks from 20 quarries

399
00:24:36,140 --> 00:24:39,540
arrived by boat at the tower docks.

400
00:24:39,640 --> 00:24:43,980
Steam-powered derricks hoisted the blocks onto land,

401
00:24:44,080 --> 00:24:48,920
and pulleys hauled them into their final position.

402
00:24:48,921 --> 00:24:52,260
Vertical wooden tracks along the sides of the towers

403
00:24:52,360 --> 00:24:56,660
supported each stone as it made its ascent.

404
00:24:56,760 --> 00:25:01,860
The huge towers were 59 feet wide and 140 feet long.

405
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:07,240
By June 1, 1872, when the Brooklyn Tower was 100 feet

406
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:11,180
high, it was still far broader than it was tall,

407
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:14,620
yet it dwarfed every building in sight.

408
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:18,520
The sheer height of the towers made the work hazardous.

409
00:25:18,521 --> 00:25:22,420
Four men died from being crushed by falling stone.

410
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:26,320
Five others were killed in falls from the towers.

411
00:25:26,420 --> 00:25:29,260
The biggest problem of workers on top of the towers

412
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:33,060
was signaling to the men below who ran the hoisting engines.

413
00:25:33,160 --> 00:25:36,680
It was too windy for shouting.

414
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:39,880
Fog made flag signals impossible,

415
00:25:39,980 --> 00:25:43,480
and signal bells often broke down.

416
00:25:43,580 --> 00:25:46,820
Men looking over the edge struggled not to be thrown

417
00:25:46,821 --> 00:25:50,720
off balance by sudden gusts of wind.

418
00:25:50,820 --> 00:25:53,620
Despite the absence of the chief engineer,

419
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:57,760
the Brooklyn Tower was finished in June 1875.

420
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:02,300
The New York Tower was ready in July 1876.

421
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:07,940
Standing 276 1 1⁄2 feet high, more than 8 feet taller

422
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:11,520
than John Roebling had projected, the Twin Towers

423
00:26:11,620 --> 00:26:14,020
dominated the skyline.

424
00:26:14,021 --> 00:26:17,820
Their glory could only be imagined by Washington Roebling

425
00:26:17,920 --> 00:26:20,220
on his sickbed in Trenton.

426
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:22,960
Not all his symptoms were caused by the Benz.

427
00:26:23,060 --> 00:26:25,060
His nervous system had been shattered

428
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:27,660
by overwork and anxiety.

429
00:26:27,760 --> 00:26:31,380
Unable to read, write, or tolerate visitors,

430
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:34,280
he was still in constant touch with his assistants,

431
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:38,380
thanks to the vigilance of his wife Emily.

432
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:44,020
From 1873 to 1883, all of Washington's living

433
00:26:44,021 --> 00:26:48,020
letters, charts, and designs were written by Emily.

434
00:26:48,021 --> 00:26:54,020
She must have had a strong role in the work that was,

435
00:26:54,021 --> 00:26:57,020
the way the work was being done.

436
00:26:57,021 --> 00:27:04,020
And she was a very important part of getting the bridge built.

437
00:27:04,021 --> 00:27:13,020
It's unlikely that if after Washington Roebling was incapacitated,

438
00:27:13,021 --> 00:27:15,780
that that bridge, that he could have finished that bridge

439
00:27:15,880 --> 00:27:17,660
without her.

440
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:21,360
With Emily's help, Washington, in Trenton,

441
00:27:21,460 --> 00:27:25,260
oversaw the construction of the bridge's anchorages.

442
00:27:25,360 --> 00:27:28,460
The anchorages looked like stumpy towers with the same

443
00:27:28,560 --> 00:27:30,880
derricks and pulleys on top.

444
00:27:30,980 --> 00:27:35,580
Deep in their interior were four iron plates, each weighing

445
00:27:35,680 --> 00:27:37,920
23 tons.

446
00:27:38,020 --> 00:27:42,120
Attached to the plates were a series of iron bars curving

447
00:27:42,121 --> 00:27:46,120
through the masonry, ready to anchor the ends of the bridge's

448
00:27:46,220 --> 00:27:49,220
four main cables.

449
00:27:49,320 --> 00:27:54,060
With the towers and anchorages complete in 1876,

450
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:57,860
workmen prepared the first traveler rope.

451
00:27:57,960 --> 00:28:03,580
A 6,800-foot-long loop of steel no thicker than a man's thumb,

452
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:06,280
the traveler would extend over the towers

453
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:08,780
and be secured at each anchorage.

454
00:28:08,781 --> 00:28:12,220
Its purpose was to haul the cable wires and spinning

455
00:28:12,320 --> 00:28:15,920
equipment back and forth.

456
00:28:16,020 --> 00:28:23,820
At 3.30 PM, August 14, 1876, 6,000 people watched and cheered

457
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:25,940
as the traveler was hung.

458
00:28:26,040 --> 00:28:29,340
New York and Brooklyn were finally physically connected

459
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:33,040
by a single loop of steel.

460
00:28:33,140 --> 00:28:37,540
The Brooklyn Eagle ran the ecstatic headline, Wedded.

461
00:28:37,541 --> 00:28:40,440
The New York Herald, playing hard to get,

462
00:28:40,540 --> 00:28:43,780
said it was only an engagement as yet.

463
00:28:43,880 --> 00:28:46,780
A telegram announcing the traveler's success

464
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:50,580
was sent from the bridge to Washington Roebling in Trenton.

465
00:28:50,680 --> 00:28:54,300
The sender was Frank Farrington.

466
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:56,800
Farrington was the master mechanic.

467
00:28:56,900 --> 00:29:05,300
And in 1876, they decided that he was going to cross the bridge

468
00:29:05,301 --> 00:29:08,200
and become the first person, really,

469
00:29:08,300 --> 00:29:11,540
to go from one tower to the other.

470
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:14,340
On August 25, Farrington was strapped

471
00:29:14,440 --> 00:29:17,080
into a bosun's chair, looped to the Traveler,

472
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:19,180
which was shunted from Brooklyn to New York

473
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:21,220
by steam-powered pulleys.

474
00:29:21,320 --> 00:29:23,460
Halfway through his 22-minute ride,

475
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:27,960
the 60-year-old Farrington made a gallant gesture.

476
00:29:28,060 --> 00:29:32,620
He took the safety rope away and stood up on the swing

477
00:29:32,621 --> 00:29:35,360
and waved to people with his hat.

478
00:29:35,460 --> 00:29:38,860
And then he sat down again, and I don't think he put his safety

479
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:43,260
belt on, but he sat down again and blew kisses

480
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:45,760
to the crowds down below.

481
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:48,160
The main reason for Farrington's ride

482
00:29:48,260 --> 00:29:50,740
was to demonstrate the safety of the traveler

483
00:29:50,840 --> 00:29:53,480
to untrained workmen.

484
00:29:53,580 --> 00:29:56,240
Unable to find experienced bridge builders,

485
00:29:56,340 --> 00:29:59,640
Farrington had hired sailors who were at least used to working

486
00:29:59,740 --> 00:30:01,180
at great heights.

487
00:30:01,181 --> 00:30:05,020
One of their first tasks was to construct a 48-inch wide,

488
00:30:05,120 --> 00:30:08,680
temporary footbridge that hung between both towers.

489
00:30:08,780 --> 00:30:12,420
The flimsy footbridge was intended for the workmen,

490
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:15,720
but it became a huge tourist attraction.

491
00:30:15,820 --> 00:30:18,540
Thousands of ordinary visitors got passes

492
00:30:18,640 --> 00:30:21,440
to cross the river by footbridge.

493
00:30:21,540 --> 00:30:24,440
Some found it a harrowing experience,

494
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:27,540
but amazingly, no one was hurt.

495
00:30:27,640 --> 00:30:30,480
If tourists were thrilled to be part of the bridge,

496
00:30:30,481 --> 00:30:34,120
Washington Roebling was tormented to be in Trenton now

497
00:30:34,220 --> 00:30:37,580
that his dream was becoming a reality.

498
00:30:37,680 --> 00:30:40,620
Throughout four years of agonizing pain,

499
00:30:40,720 --> 00:30:45,220
he had not once seen the bridge he lost his health to create.

500
00:30:45,320 --> 00:30:48,040
But now the cable spinning was about to begin.

501
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:50,540
Cabling, the work he liked best,

502
00:30:50,640 --> 00:30:53,440
would make this a real bridge at last,

503
00:30:53,540 --> 00:30:56,740
and Washington Roebling could no longer stay away.

504
00:31:00,480 --> 00:31:04,280
In October 1876, Washington and Emily Roebling

505
00:31:04,380 --> 00:31:06,620
returned to Brooklyn by boat.

506
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:09,920
Washington was too frail to go to the site.

507
00:31:10,020 --> 00:31:12,060
His view of the bridge was limited

508
00:31:12,160 --> 00:31:14,360
to the range of his binoculars as he

509
00:31:14,460 --> 00:31:17,200
peered from the window of his new house a half mile

510
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:18,900
from the Brooklyn Bridge.

511
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:22,540
Although housebound, Washington continued

512
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:24,540
to manage the building of the bridge

513
00:31:24,640 --> 00:31:27,380
thanks to his direct link to the construction process.

514
00:31:27,381 --> 00:31:31,080
thanks to his direct link to the construction process, Emily.

515
00:31:34,080 --> 00:31:39,620
Once Washington was incapacitated and unable to personally go

516
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:41,820
to oversee the construction of the Brooklyn Bridge,

517
00:31:41,920 --> 00:31:43,720
she would go to the bridge for him,

518
00:31:43,820 --> 00:31:45,940
and he would simply watch the construction from his window,

519
00:31:46,040 --> 00:31:47,940
which I believe was situated somewhere over here

520
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:50,340
in Brooklyn Heights, where he lived.

521
00:31:50,440 --> 00:31:52,140
And she would go actually to the site

522
00:31:52,240 --> 00:31:53,640
and bring his orders to the workers

523
00:31:53,740 --> 00:31:55,540
so that the bridge could be completed.

524
00:31:55,541 --> 00:31:58,740
With Emily at his side, Washington supervised

525
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:01,340
the spinning of the bridge's cables.

526
00:32:01,440 --> 00:32:04,740
The process was called spinning because a wheel,

527
00:32:04,840 --> 00:32:08,080
as in spinning wheel, carried the wire back and forth

528
00:32:08,180 --> 00:32:09,780
across the river.

529
00:32:09,880 --> 00:32:11,880
It looks as if it's being spun.

530
00:32:11,980 --> 00:32:17,200
There's a wheel that carries the wires across,

531
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:19,700
and the wheel turns.

532
00:32:19,800 --> 00:32:22,600
But the wires are straight.

533
00:32:22,700 --> 00:32:25,140
They're all parallel wires.

534
00:32:25,141 --> 00:32:30,140
And they picked up at one end, brought over to the other end,

535
00:32:30,240 --> 00:32:33,440
taken off, and put on the anchors.

536
00:32:33,540 --> 00:32:37,220
A compressed bundle of 282 parallel wires

537
00:32:37,320 --> 00:32:39,260
was called a strand.

538
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:43,560
There were 19 strands, or 3,500 miles, of wire

539
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:46,920
in each of the bridge's four cables.

540
00:32:47,020 --> 00:32:49,200
Fastening the strands to the anchor bars

541
00:32:49,300 --> 00:32:51,860
was the most dangerous part of cabling

542
00:32:51,861 --> 00:32:55,940
because of the 75-ton strain on each strand.

543
00:32:56,040 --> 00:33:00,440
On June 14, 1878, Frank Farrington and four others

544
00:33:00,540 --> 00:33:03,200
were attaching a strand at the New York anchorage

545
00:33:03,300 --> 00:33:07,240
when a steel rope holding the strand snapped.

546
00:33:07,340 --> 00:33:11,380
It killed one man instantly and flung another, who later died,

547
00:33:11,480 --> 00:33:13,380
80 feet into the yard.

548
00:33:13,480 --> 00:33:15,920
Newspapers blamed Washington because the rope

549
00:33:16,020 --> 00:33:20,480
was a Roebling product, but the rope was not defective.

550
00:33:20,481 --> 00:33:23,980
However, defective metal was used on the bridge.

551
00:33:24,080 --> 00:33:27,920
It was supplied by a Brooklyn wire maker, J. Lloyd Hay,

552
00:33:28,020 --> 00:33:31,720
who manufactured all of the 7 million pounds of wire

553
00:33:31,820 --> 00:33:33,720
in the strands.

554
00:33:33,820 --> 00:33:37,160
In July 1878, Washington announced

555
00:33:37,260 --> 00:33:41,840
that Hay had bribed inspectors and replaced good wire with bad.

556
00:33:41,940 --> 00:33:43,840
Fortunately, the bridge was designed

557
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:46,680
to be six times stronger than needed.

558
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:48,780
Washington felt it was still safe,

559
00:33:48,781 --> 00:33:52,660
but he insisted that Hay supply good wire to make up for the bad

560
00:33:52,760 --> 00:33:54,780
wires already in place.

561
00:33:54,880 --> 00:33:59,760
Hay agreed and was never penalized for this fraud.

562
00:33:59,860 --> 00:34:02,000
Washington had to accept that.

563
00:34:02,100 --> 00:34:06,000
His one-man rule had ended in 1874 when the bridge was

564
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:10,600
redefined as a public work run by a board of trustees.

565
00:34:10,700 --> 00:34:13,480
Construction costs were paid by the city of Brooklyn

566
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:18,340
and not paid by the city of New York, which by mid-1877

567
00:34:18,341 --> 00:34:21,780
owed half a million dollars to the bridge.

568
00:34:21,880 --> 00:34:25,440
Claiming the bridge was unsafe, New York shrugged and said,

569
00:34:25,540 --> 00:34:27,080
so sue us.

570
00:34:27,180 --> 00:34:29,480
The bridge trustees had no choice.

571
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:33,980
In the fall of 1878, they ran out of money and filed suit.

572
00:34:34,080 --> 00:34:38,900
The courts forced New York to pay up in May the following year.

573
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,600
Meanwhile, building continued but moved slowly

574
00:34:41,700 --> 00:34:43,740
due to bureaucratic delays.

575
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:47,940
In 1879, steel suspenders were hung from the cables.

576
00:34:47,941 --> 00:34:52,180
It would be another two years before the 8,000 pound steel

577
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:54,680
floor beams were all in place.

578
00:34:54,780 --> 00:34:59,080
So little work had remained, yet it had taken so much time.

579
00:34:59,180 --> 00:35:02,380
Deliveries of supplies were way overdue,

580
00:35:02,480 --> 00:35:04,900
perhaps because payments were too.

581
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:07,000
It took all of Emily Roebling's tact

582
00:35:07,100 --> 00:35:10,000
to keep the project moving.

583
00:35:10,100 --> 00:35:16,300
Possibly her greatest skill was able to calm people's

584
00:35:16,301 --> 00:35:19,640
emotions, whenever there was some turmoil coming in,

585
00:35:19,740 --> 00:35:23,100
she'd go over and talk to them, make them feel good,

586
00:35:23,200 --> 00:35:25,240
that sort of diplomacy.

587
00:35:25,340 --> 00:35:28,240
Years later, Emily told her son John,

588
00:35:28,340 --> 00:35:33,020
I have more brains, common sense, and know-how generally

589
00:35:33,120 --> 00:35:38,060
than any two engineers, civil or uncivil, that I have ever met.

590
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:40,460
And but for me, the Brooklyn Bridge

591
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:42,560
would never have had the name Roebling

592
00:35:42,660 --> 00:35:45,100
in any way connected with it.

593
00:35:45,101 --> 00:35:53,100
Emily's high visibility led to gossip that her husband was a bit crazy, unable to speak, and had lost his grip.

594
00:35:53,101 --> 00:35:54,100
This was untrue.

595
00:35:54,101 --> 00:35:57,100
Washington was very much in his right mind.

596
00:35:57,101 --> 00:36:00,100
As always, that mind was on the bridge.

597
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:08,100
In late 1881, Washington asked the trustees for another 1,000 tons of steel to stiffen the roadway.

598
00:36:08,101 --> 00:36:10,100
An uproar ensued.

599
00:36:10,101 --> 00:36:11,840
An uproar ensued.

600
00:36:11,940 --> 00:36:17,100
The bridge had cost $13,500,000, more than twice

601
00:36:17,200 --> 00:36:18,940
the original estimate.

602
00:36:19,040 --> 00:36:23,940
Washington's request seemed outrageous, even to engineers.

603
00:36:24,040 --> 00:36:26,580
In fact, well into the 20th century,

604
00:36:26,680 --> 00:36:30,360
engineers were still trying to economize on steel

605
00:36:30,460 --> 00:36:32,760
and were building unstable bridges.

606
00:36:32,860 --> 00:36:37,660
This was illustrated in 1940, when a 45 mile an hour wind

607
00:36:37,661 --> 00:36:43,260
lifted the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington state to its dramatic destruction.

608
00:36:49,020 --> 00:36:51,107
History proved Washington right.

609
00:36:51,108 --> 00:36:55,020
Without more steel, the bridge's roadway would not have been

610
00:36:55,021 --> 00:37:00,844
stiff enough to carry the automotive traffic that arrived in the next century.

611
00:37:00,845 --> 00:37:01,740
But in 1881,

612
00:37:01,741 --> 00:37:06,960
Additional costs were intolerable to trustees who had no loyalty to Washington Roebling.

613
00:37:07,980 --> 00:37:09,560
Half the board had never seen him.

614
00:37:10,320 --> 00:37:12,660
None had seen him in the past eight years.

615
00:37:13,180 --> 00:37:15,320
His sanity was viciously attacked.

616
00:37:17,560 --> 00:37:20,000
Washington Roebling's job was in jeopardy.

617
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:25,300
Never before had so many powerful people, including the mayors of New York and Brooklyn,

618
00:37:25,301 --> 00:37:27,360
sought his exit from the project.

619
00:37:31,740 --> 00:37:35,740
By 1882, Washington Roebling's 13-year battle to build his

620
00:37:35,840 --> 00:37:40,740
bridge had wrecked his health, sapped his energy, slandered

621
00:37:40,840 --> 00:37:44,480
his reputation, made him a recluse, and the Brooklyn

622
00:37:44,580 --> 00:37:47,280
Bridge still wasn't finished.

623
00:37:47,380 --> 00:37:49,900
But the roadway was in place.

624
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:53,500
And in June, the state legislature ordered New York

625
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:58,000
and Brooklyn to pay whatever it cost for the city.

626
00:37:58,001 --> 00:38:04,400
New York and Brooklyn to pay whatever it cost to complete the bridge as Washington requested.

627
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:10,640
But the political candidates on the Board of Trustees wanted to win votes by having the

628
00:38:10,641 --> 00:38:13,567
bridge open before the upcoming election.

629
00:38:13,568 --> 00:38:17,280
They required Washington to appear before them to be

630
00:38:17,281 --> 00:38:19,427
admonished. He refused.

631
00:38:19,428 --> 00:38:25,680
He wrote the trustees that the bridge wouldn't be ready until 1883.

632
00:38:25,681 --> 00:38:30,680
Then he and Emily left town for their first vacation in five years.

633
00:38:30,780 --> 00:38:34,680
They went to Newport, Rhode Island.

634
00:38:34,780 --> 00:38:37,920
Seth Lowe, the mayor of Brooklyn, was outraged.

635
00:38:38,020 --> 00:38:42,740
If Washington Roebling couldn't or wouldn't open the bridge this year,

636
00:38:42,840 --> 00:38:45,640
why not get rid of him?

637
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:51,640
In July 1882, Seth Lowe arrived at Washington's home in Newport,

638
00:38:51,641 --> 00:38:56,640
unexpected, unwelcome, and certainly uninvited.

639
00:38:56,740 --> 00:39:00,040
With no authority except his own ambition,

640
00:39:00,140 --> 00:39:04,180
Lowe demanded Washington's resignation as chief engineer.

641
00:39:04,280 --> 00:39:07,260
Washington said the trustees would have to fire him.

642
00:39:07,360 --> 00:39:10,220
Seth Lowe said they'd try.

643
00:39:10,320 --> 00:39:12,820
New York Mayor William Grace supported

644
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:16,000
Mayor Lowe's resolution to replace Washington.

645
00:39:16,100 --> 00:39:18,960
For once, the mayors of New York and Brooklyn

646
00:39:18,961 --> 00:39:22,400
and Brooklyn agreed on a matter concerning the bridge.

647
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:26,360
Had the trustees voted then, Washington would have lost.

648
00:39:26,460 --> 00:39:28,140
But they waited.

649
00:39:28,240 --> 00:39:31,140
Emily anxiously sought allies to spread the word

650
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:33,640
that no one on Washington's loyal staff

651
00:39:33,740 --> 00:39:36,920
would agree to replace him.

652
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:40,020
Washington Roebling didn't care about allies.

653
00:39:40,120 --> 00:39:42,960
He told a reporter the trustees would say anything

654
00:39:43,060 --> 00:39:45,520
to serve their political careers.

655
00:39:45,620 --> 00:39:48,420
In September, by a vote of 10 to 7,

656
00:39:48,421 --> 00:39:51,760
Washington Roebling wasn't fired.

657
00:39:51,860 --> 00:39:53,920
Seth Lowe immediately shifted gears

658
00:39:54,020 --> 00:39:56,760
and said he'd hoped Washington would win.

659
00:39:56,860 --> 00:40:01,960
Not surprisingly, Seth Lowe had a long political career.

660
00:40:02,060 --> 00:40:06,080
Driven by carriage in 1883, Washington felt well enough

661
00:40:06,180 --> 00:40:08,080
to be driven to the half-finished Brooklyn

662
00:40:08,180 --> 00:40:09,680
Terminal in person.

663
00:40:09,780 --> 00:40:12,420
He made his observations from the carriage window

664
00:40:12,520 --> 00:40:15,720
and stayed inside the vehicle.

665
00:40:15,721 --> 00:40:19,260
By early May, Washington was ready to test the effect

666
00:40:19,360 --> 00:40:22,420
of a trotting horse on the roadway.

667
00:40:22,520 --> 00:40:25,960
Washington gave Emily that honor.

668
00:40:26,060 --> 00:40:28,460
As people cheered, Emily and her coachman

669
00:40:28,560 --> 00:40:33,080
became the first people to cross the Brooklyn Bridge.

670
00:40:33,180 --> 00:40:35,580
Inside the carriage, Emily held a rooster

671
00:40:35,680 --> 00:40:37,480
as a symbol of victory.

672
00:40:37,580 --> 00:40:40,680
But she was the one who deserved to crow.

673
00:40:40,780 --> 00:40:45,020
It had taken more than 14 years, nearly 40 lives,

674
00:40:45,021 --> 00:40:49,320
and over $15 million, but the Brooklyn Bridge opened at last

675
00:40:49,420 --> 00:40:52,360
on May 24, 1883.

676
00:40:52,460 --> 00:40:55,260
It was decreed the People's Day.

677
00:40:55,360 --> 00:40:58,460
The entire city celebrated mankind's capacity

678
00:40:58,560 --> 00:41:02,580
for invention embodied in this wondrous bridge leading

679
00:41:02,680 --> 00:41:06,280
from the limitations of the past to the infinite possibilities

680
00:41:06,380 --> 00:41:07,180
of the future.

681
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:13,780
The day began with the parade of 7,000 dignitaries

682
00:41:13,781 --> 00:41:18,320
headed by the guest of honor, President Chester A. Arthur.

683
00:41:18,420 --> 00:41:22,180
They called him the Apollo of America here.

684
00:41:22,280 --> 00:41:25,420
Good looking man in terms of that particular time.

685
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:27,820
And everybody turned out to see him.

686
00:41:27,920 --> 00:41:29,520
The other interesting person was there

687
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:31,340
was the governor of New York, Grover Cleveland,

688
00:41:31,440 --> 00:41:34,040
who became a later president.

689
00:41:34,140 --> 00:41:35,840
And then they came down to this Manhattan

690
00:41:35,940 --> 00:41:39,680
end of the bridge here, where the president got out

691
00:41:39,780 --> 00:41:43,780
and walked across the bridge to meet Mayor Seth Lowe of Brooklyn

692
00:41:43,781 --> 00:41:48,780
Afterward, over 150,000 people from every walk of life

693
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:51,720
were welcomed to stroll across the bridge

694
00:41:51,820 --> 00:41:55,720
for the price of a penny apiece.

695
00:41:55,820 --> 00:41:59,320
The only person of consequence who stayed away from the bridge

696
00:41:59,420 --> 00:42:01,440
was Washington Roebling.

697
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:04,640
He watched the events from the window of his home.

698
00:42:04,740 --> 00:42:09,180
But Emily insisted that Washington receive his proper acclaim.

699
00:42:09,280 --> 00:42:12,880
That afternoon, she hosted a private reception

700
00:42:12,881 --> 00:42:16,980
President Arthur, Governor Cleveland, and 1,000 other guests

701
00:42:17,080 --> 00:42:20,680
came to the Roebling home to honor the Bridges' founding

702
00:42:20,780 --> 00:42:22,320
family.

703
00:42:22,420 --> 00:42:25,620
Through struggle, suffering, and sacrifice,

704
00:42:25,720 --> 00:42:29,340
Washington and Emily Roebling breathed life into the child

705
00:42:29,440 --> 00:42:33,200
born of John Roebling's genius, then clothed it in steel and

706
00:42:33,300 --> 00:42:37,280
granite to fulfill its destiny as the greatest engineering

707
00:42:37,380 --> 00:42:39,940
work of its age.

708
00:42:39,941 --> 00:42:45,740
On May 25, 1883, Washington and Emily left for Newport.

709
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:49,980
Within six weeks, Washington resigned as chief engineer.

710
00:42:50,080 --> 00:42:52,580
He'd invested a full third of his life

711
00:42:52,680 --> 00:42:54,880
in building the Brooklyn Bridge.

712
00:42:54,980 --> 00:42:57,600
He never wanted to build another.

713
00:42:57,700 --> 00:43:02,100
Instead, he built Emily the mansion of her dreams in Trenton.

714
00:43:02,200 --> 00:43:05,840
She fulfilled another dream when she became a lawyer a few

715
00:43:05,841 --> 00:43:11,240
a few years before her death at the age of 55 in 1903.

716
00:43:11,340 --> 00:43:15,180
Washington outlived her by more than 20 years.

717
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:19,980
At 84, he took over the Roebling Wire Empire,

718
00:43:20,080 --> 00:43:23,700
making it the biggest, most prosperous in the world.

719
00:43:23,800 --> 00:43:30,500
He stayed on the job until his death five years later in 1926.

720
00:43:30,600 --> 00:43:33,100
The bridge that now honors the Roebling's achievements

721
00:43:33,101 --> 00:43:37,100
on its own, but it didn't stand alone for long.

722
00:43:37,200 --> 00:43:38,940
After the turn of the century, there

723
00:43:39,040 --> 00:43:41,000
were three more bridges to Brooklyn,

724
00:43:41,100 --> 00:43:44,140
and eventually over a dozen tunnels.

725
00:43:44,240 --> 00:43:47,140
Yet only the Brooklyn Bridge seems to reach out

726
00:43:47,240 --> 00:43:50,760
and communicate a uniquely American message,

727
00:43:50,860 --> 00:43:55,960
born of the toil, struggle, and soaring vision spun into every

728
00:43:56,060 --> 00:43:58,700
wire of its web of steel.

729
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:02,060
The Brooklyn Bridge was a handmade bridge.

730
00:44:02,061 --> 00:44:08,960
which to me is incredible considering its size and its beauty.

731
00:44:09,060 --> 00:44:12,800
I think it's just classic because of the Gothic look

732
00:44:12,900 --> 00:44:14,200
of the bridge itself.

733
00:44:14,300 --> 00:44:18,080
It's so much a part of New York and so much a part, certainly,

734
00:44:18,180 --> 00:44:19,540
of Brooklyn.

735
00:44:19,640 --> 00:44:23,720
And so much a part of America's greatness.

736
00:44:23,820 --> 00:44:27,720
It took 14 years to deliver the Brooklyn Bridge, years that

737
00:44:27,820 --> 00:44:29,860
coincide with America's emergence

738
00:44:29,861 --> 00:44:33,960
as an industrial leader and a beacon of hope, a miracle

739
00:44:34,060 --> 00:44:35,500
of timing.

740
00:44:35,600 --> 00:44:39,200
Had the bridge been completed seven years before on schedule,

741
00:44:39,300 --> 00:44:41,840
it would have lacked the later technology that prepared it

742
00:44:41,940 --> 00:44:45,480
for the long cycle of service ahead.

743
00:44:45,580 --> 00:44:48,440
Had the bridge been abandoned until the next century,

744
00:44:48,540 --> 00:44:51,620
it could not have become a soaring symbol of faith

745
00:44:51,720 --> 00:44:55,560
to welcome the great wave of immigrants who came to America

746
00:44:55,660 --> 00:44:58,320
in the 1880s and 90s.

747
00:44:58,321 --> 00:45:02,160
In its superb timing, as in all its proportions,

748
00:45:02,260 --> 00:45:05,620
the Brooklyn Bridge forever entwines the solid fulfillment

749
00:45:05,720 --> 00:45:09,860
of technology with the eternal yearning of the Spirit.

